<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1515">1515</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Alaüddevle Bozkurt Bey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt was bey of the Dulkadirids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> principality around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Maraş</a>, from 1480 to 1515. He balanced between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>'s victory at Chaldiran he was defeated and killed by the Ottomans at Turnadağ in 1515, after which the principality came under Ottoman control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-bozkurt.jpg" alt="Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt" width="320" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akkoyunlu_Elvend_Mirz%C3%A2%E2%80%99n%C4%B1n_Ala%C3%BCddevle_Bey%E2%80%99in_huzuruna_gelmesi_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bozkurt was born in 1428 to Suleiman Beg, a ruler of Dulkadir, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> principality founded by Zayn al-Din Qaraja as a client of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>. After the death of his brother Malik Arslan in 1465, his brothers Shah Budak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-suwar">Shah Suwar</a> fought over the throne with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Ottoman backing. In 1467 Bozkurt married his daughter Ayshe Hatun to Bayezid, son of the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. After a raid around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> in 1479 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to appeal to Mehmed II, who made him sanjak-bey of Chirmen and later of Kırşehir and supported his claim against Shah Budak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bozkurt won the Mamluk Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a>'s recognition in February 1481 and tried to stay on good terms with both powers. He helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> hunt his brother Cem Sultan, but his wavering loyalty fed the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Mamluks: on 12 March 1485 he failed to send the aid he had promised the Ottomans on the Seyhan River, and they were defeated. In 1488 he again held back while the Mamluks beat the Ottomans near Ağaçayırı, and he married a daughter to the Mamluk commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>. The historian Cihan Yüksel argues that the usual blame placed on Bozkurt understates Bayezid's own part in these conflicts.</p><p>He refused the Safavid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>'s wish to marry his daughter, backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and briefly took towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Urfa</a> and Diyarbekir, but suffered heavy defeats by Ismail's commander Mohammad Khan Ustajlu, and two of his sons died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>. He later made peace with Ismail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bozkurt sent no greetings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> on his accession, refused on the grounds of his age to join the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, and placed an embargo on food and fodder from Ottoman lands. After his victory over Ismail at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a> in 1514, Selim turned against him, and Bozkurt was killed at the Battle of Turnadağ on 13 June 1515; his nephew Ali succeeded him.</p><p>His code of law, the Ala al-Dawla Kanunname, is the only known law code of Dulkadirid rule; it combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> with local custom and used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timar</a> system as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> did. Inscriptions call him sultan and just king. He built widely, including the Hatuniye Külliye in Marash for his consort Shamsa Khatun, the Bektutiye Mosque and the Alaüddevle Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Aintab</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt</b>, a long-reigning ruler, built in Marash and resisted the rising Safavids.</blockquote><a href="/state/dulkadirids">Dulkadirids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.2,37.18&amp;year=1505" aria-label="Elbistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5481 4286h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5481 4286h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.2,37.18&amp;year=1505">What was here in 1505? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
